     "text": [
      "**ED TRAUMA H&P:**\n",
      "This 79-year-old male was brought to the trauma bay via EMS after a significant fall from a height of approximately 15 feet while working on his roof. Per EMS report, he was found alert but confused at the scene by family and complained of diffuse body pain. On arrival, initial vital signs were heart rate 97 bpm, blood pressure 120/78 mmHg (MAP 92 mmHg), respiratory rate 22 breaths/min, and oxygen saturation 95% on a 4L nasal cannula, later titrated to 6L to maintain saturation >94%.\n",
      "\n",
      "Primary survey was completed rapidly per ATLS protocol. Airway was patent and protected. Breath sounds were clear bilaterally, though shallow, with no obvious respiratory distress. Cardiovascularly, peripheral pulses were palpable and strong, skin was warm and dry, capillary refill brisk. Neurologically, he presented with a GCS of 14 (E4V4M6), oriented to person but confused to place and time, without obvious focal deficits upon initial assessment. Gross deformities were noted to his right chest wall and left pelvic region. FAST exam was negative. Given the high-energy mechanism and initial presentation, the patient was deemed a Level 1 trauma activation. Two large-bore IVs were established, initial labs sent, and he received a 1L crystalloid bolus in the ED.\n",
      "\n",
      "CT imaging from head to pelvis demonstrated multiple injuries consistent with severe blunt trauma, including comminuted bilateral rib fractures (R: 5-9, L: 6-8), a nondisplaced pelvic rami fracture on the left, a T10 compression fracture, and a small, stable right frontal subarachnoid hemorrhage. No other acute intracranial pathologies or intra-abdominal solid organ injuries were identified. Given his age, significant injury burden, and the subarachnoid hemorrhage, the decision was made for admission to the Trauma ICU for close monitoring and management.\n",
      "\n",
      "**ICU DAY 1 PROGRESS NOTE:**\n",
      "On ICU Day 1, the patient remained hemodynamically tenuous, requiring careful fluid management. His blood pressure fluctuated, largely responsive to crystalloid boluses totaling 2L, without the need for vasopressor support. Persistent mild confusion (GCS 13-14) was noted, prompting q2h neuro checks, which remained stable without pupillary changes or worsening focal deficits. Pain was significant due to his extensive fractures and was managed with a multimodal analgesia strategy, including scheduled acetaminophen, gabapentin, and titrated IV hydromorphone boluses, which provided reasonable comfort while avoiding oversedation. Ventilatory support included supplemental oxygen via nasal cannula, maintaining SpO2 >92% with a respiratory rate ranging from 18-24 breaths/min. Aggressive pulmonary toileting with incentive spirometry was initiated. Invasive monitoring with an arterial line was placed for continuous blood pressure surveillance. Strict input/output monitoring was maintained via Foley catheter. Initial laboratory trends showed a stable hemoglobin and creatinine. Prophylactic low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) and proton pump inhibitor (PPI) were initiated.\n",
      "\n",
      "**ICU DAY 2 PROGRESS NOTE:**\n",
      "The patient's overall status showed gradual improvement on ICU Day 2. Hemodynamically, he became more stable, no longer requiring fluid boluses, and his blood pressure maintained within target range. Respiratory function continued to be a focus; his oxygen requirements remained at 4L NC, and he tolerated periods off oxygen with close monitoring. Chest physiotherapy and incentive spirometry efforts were reinforced. Cardiovascular monitoring remained crucial, with no new arrhythmias or signs of cardiac strain. Renal function remained stable with adequate urine output (average 0.8 ml/kg/hr) and stable creatinine. A comprehensive review of his injury pattern confirmed the decision for non-operative management of his rib, pelvic, and vertebral fractures. Empiric antibiotic therapy, initially started due to the high-energy mechanism and potential for occult injuries, was reassessed based on negative cultures and clinical stability, and subsequently discontinued. Venous thromboembolism prophylaxis with LMWH and sequential compression devices (SCDs) was confirmed as appropriate and continued. Early enteral nutrition was initiated via nasogastric tube, with good tolerance.\n",
      "\n",
      "**ICU DAY 3 PROGRESS NOTE:**\n",
      "ICU Day 3 marked significant progress towards stabilization. He was hemodynamically stable without any pressor support or fluid boluses, maintaining mean arterial pressures consistently above 80 mmHg. Respiratory efforts continued to improve, allowing for successful weaning to room air for prolonged periods. His pain was well-controlled with oral analgesics (oxycodone and acetaminophen) and he was more alert and oriented, with GCS consistently at 15. The team focused on early mobilization; physical therapy initiated bedside evaluations and assisted the patient out of bed to a chair for short periods, which he tolerated well. He was able to participate in basic activities of daily living with assistance. Given his sustained hemodynamic stability, improving respiratory function, stable neurological status, and manageable pain control, the patient was deemed eligible for transfer to the trauma ward for continued recovery.\n",
      "\n",
      "**WARD / FLOOR COURSE:**\n",
      "Following transfer out of the ICU, the patient continued a remarkable recovery on the trauma ward. Pain control transitioned smoothly to an oral regimen, which was effectively managed with a combination of scheduled Tylenol and PRN oxycodone. Physical therapy became more intensive, focusing on ambulation, strengthening exercises, and improving his range of motion. He progressed from walking with a rolling walker for short distances to ambulating independently with a cane by the end of his ward stay. Occupational therapy worked on adaptive strategies for ADLs, which he quickly mastered.\n",
      "\n",
      "Close monitoring for hospital-acquired complications was emphasized throughout his ward course. Nursing notes consistently described his gradual improvement in mobility and increasing participation in self-care. He remained afebrile, without signs of respiratory compromise or new neurological deficits. Daily assessments for deep vein thrombosis (DVT), pneumonia, urinary tract infection (UTI), and delirium were consistently negative. His skin integrity remained intact, and bowel function normalized. Nutritional intake was robust, and he regained his appetite. Social work collaborated closely with the patient and family to plan for a safe discharge, arranging for home health services and necessary equipment.\n",
      "\n",
      "**DISCHARGE SUMMARY:**\n",
      "At the time of discharge, this 79-year-old male demonstrated an excellent recovery from his significant fall from height and associated injuries, which included multiple bilateral rib fractures, a nondisplaced left pelvic rami fracture, a T10 compression fracture, and a small subarachnoid hemorrhage. His entire hospital course was managed non-operatively, highlighting his resilience and the effectiveness of conservative treatment for his specific injury pattern. He successfully navigated his critical care stay without requiring prolonged mechanical ventilation or vasopressor support, and his neurological status returned to baseline. No significant complications, such as pneumonia, DVT/PE, delirium, or skin breakdown, were encountered throughout his hospitalization. He was discharged home with his family, ambulating independently with a cane, tolerating a regular diet, and managing his pain effectively with oral medications. A detailed discharge medication list and care instructions were provided. Follow-up appointments were arranged with Trauma Surgery for overall injury management, Orthopedic Surgery for his pelvic and vertebral fractures, and a dedicated outpatient physical therapy program to ensure continued strength and mobility gains.\n"
     ]
